The Security Council,
Recalling all of its previous resolutions, statements of its President, and press statements on the situation in the Central African Republic (CAR), including resolution 2759 (2024),
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the CAR, and recalling the importance of the principles of non-interference, good-neighbourliness and regional cooperation,
Reaffirming the basic principles of peacekeeping, such as consent of the parties, impartiality, and non-use of force, except in self-defence and defence of the mandate, and recognising that the mandate of each peacekeeping mission is specific to the need and situation of the country concerned,
Recalling that the CAR Government has the primary responsibility to protect all populations in the CAR, including from international crimes, recognising the persistent security challenge threatening civilians, and underlining the progress made to restore State authority in all parts of the country, including through MINUSCA’s assistance,
Emphasising that any sustainable solution to the crisis in the CAR should be CAR-owned, including the political process, and should prioritise reconciliation of the Central African people through an inclusive process that involves men and women of all social, economic, political, religious and ethnic backgrounds, including those displaced by the crisis,
Recalling the signing of the Political Agreement on Peace and Reconciliation in the CAR by the CAR Government and fourteen armed groups in Bangui on 6 February 2019 (“the APPR”), stressing its centrality and the need for further progress in its implementation, and reaffirming that implementation of the APPR remains the only mechanism for achieving lasting peace and stability in the CAR and the only framework for dialogue with armed groups that have renounced violence, and welcoming the dissolution, disarmament and demobilization of six armed groups and of the wings of three other armed groups out of fourteen signatories of the APPR,
Condemning in the strongest terms violations of the APPR and violence perpetrated by all parties to the conflict, particularly armed groups, throughout the country, including attacks against civilians, peacekeepers, United Nations personnel, national security and defence forces, and humanitarian actors, and urging all armed groups to cease immediately all forms of violence and all signatories to the APPR to respect their commitments in full,
Condemning in particular the continued attacks by armed groups, including the Coalition of Patriots for Change, the Union for Peace in the Central African Republic, 3R and other groups, including recent attacks in Vakaga, Haute-Kotto, Ouham-Pendé and elsewhere, and expressing serious concern at cross-border criminal activities, arms trafficking, the illicit exploitation and trade of natural resources, and the use of mercenaries and violations of international humanitarian law and human rights abuses perpetrated by them,
Recalling its resolutions on Women, Peace and Security, Youth, Peace and Security, Children and Armed Conflict, and the protection of civilians in armed conflict, and expressing serious concern at violations and abuses against children and conflict-related sexual violence,
Stressing the urgent and imperative need to end impunity in the CAR, noting the ongoing work of the Special Criminal Court, welcoming the 24 July 2025 judgments of the International Criminal Court in the case against Patrice-Edouard Ngaïssona and Alfred Yekatom as an important step in the fight against impunity, and calling on the CAR Government to swiftly investigate allegations of violations and to bring to justice perpetrators of violations of international humanitarian law and violations and abuses of human rights,
Welcoming the CAR Government’s continued engagement with regional and international partners of the peace process, including the African Union, the Economic Community of Central African States, the International Conference of the Great Lakes Region and its roadmap adopted in Luanda on 16 September 2021, and underlining the need for coherent, transparent and effective coordination of international support to the CAR, including by France, the Russian Federation, the United States of America, China, Rwanda, the European Union and other partners,
Underlining the continued and crucial need to support national efforts towards the extension of State authority and the reform of the security sector, which contributes to security and justice for all through independent, accountable and functioning justice and security institutions, which take into account women’s full, equal, meaningful and safe participation, as well as capacity-building of security institutions in the CAR to assume full responsibility for its security,
Expressing serious concern about the dire humanitarian situation in the CAR, including the impact of the crisis in the Sudan on the humanitarian and security situation, particularly in the border area, condemning attacks against humanitarian and medical personnel and obstruction of humanitarian access, and emphasising the current humanitarian needs of almost half of the population of the country, including civilians under threat from violence and the alarming situation of internally displaced persons and refugees,
Recognising the adverse effects of climate change, ecological changes and natural disasters on the stability of the Central African region, including through drought, desertification, land degradation, food insecurity and energy access, and stressing the need for comprehensive risk assessment and long-term strategies to build resilience,
Having considered the report of the Secretary-General on the situation in the Central African Republic,
Determining that the situation in the Central African Republic continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security in the region,
Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,
1. Decides to extend the mandate of the United Nations Multidimensional Integrated Stabilization Mission in the Central African Republic (MINUSCA) until 15 November 2026, within its current authorized strength and resources;
2. Decides that MINUSCA’s mandate shall include the following priority tasks:
(a) to protect civilians under threat of physical violence, including through a robust and proactive posture, mobile patrols, early warning mechanisms, community liaison and coordination with national authorities, and to protect United Nations personnel, humanitarian actors and civilians in areas of deployment;
(b) to provide good offices and support for the implementation of the APPR and the peace process, including through the ICGLR roadmap, local peace committees, inclusive dialogue, reconciliation and the full, equal and meaningful participation of women and youth;
(c) to support the extension of State authority throughout the territory of the CAR, including in the areas of justice, corrections, border management and the rule of law, in coordination with the CAR Government and international partners;
(d) to support security sector reform, including by providing training, mentoring, technical advice and capacity-building to the CAR defence and security forces, police, gendarmerie and corrections personnel, with a view to enabling them to assume their responsibilities in full respect for human rights and international humanitarian law;
(e) to promote and protect human rights, monitor, investigate and report on violations and abuses of international humanitarian law and human rights, including those against children and conflict-related sexual violence, and to support the fight against impunity, including through support to the Special Criminal Court and national judicial authorities;
(f) to contribute to the creation of a secure environment for the safe, timely and unhindered delivery of humanitarian assistance and to facilitate access by humanitarian actors, in accordance with humanitarian principles;
3. Authorizes MINUSCA to use all necessary means, within the limits of its capacities and areas of deployment, to carry out its mandate, including the protection of civilians, the protection of United Nations personnel and humanitarian actors, and support to the extension of State authority;
4. Requests MINUSCA to maintain a robust posture and proactive deployment, including in hotspots and areas where civilians are at risk, and to enhance early warning, community liaison and coordination with the CAR authorities and international partners;
5. Calls upon the CAR Government and all armed groups to cooperate fully with MINUSCA, ensure the safety, security and freedom of movement of United Nations personnel and humanitarian actors, and immediately cease all attacks and acts of obstruction against them;
6. Requests MINUSCA to support the implementation of the APPR and the broader peace process, including disarmament, demobilization and reintegration, community violence reduction and reconciliation initiatives, in coordination with the African Union, the Economic Community of Central African States, the International Conference of the Great Lakes Region, the European Union, the World Bank and other partners;
7. Calls upon the CAR authorities to ensure inclusive, credible and peaceful elections, including through the full, equal and meaningful participation of women and youth, and to guarantee freedom of expression, assembly and political participation, and requests MINUSCA, within existing resources and upon request, to support the electoral process;
8. Emphasises that all parties must comply with international humanitarian law and human rights law, and that United Nations support to the CAR defence and security forces shall be consistent with the Human Rights Due Diligence Policy;
9. Requests MINUSCA to support the Special Criminal Court and national judicial authorities in the fight against impunity, to implement child protection commitments, including the protocol between the CAR Government and the United Nations on the protection and transfer of children associated with armed groups and armed forces to civilian authorities, and to monitor and report on violations and abuses against children and conflict-related sexual violence;
10. Requests MINUSCA to support humanitarian access and the protection of humanitarian personnel, and to coordinate closely with humanitarian actors to facilitate the safe delivery of assistance;
11. Calls upon all Member States and international partners to provide coordinated support to MINUSCA and to the CAR authorities, including through the European Union Training Mission in the CAR, the European Union Advisory Mission in the CAR and other partners, in accordance with relevant Security Council resolutions and with full respect for human rights and international humanitarian law;
12. Requests the Secretary-General to conduct a strategic review of MINUSCA by 31 May 2026, including recommendations on the mandate, configuration and benchmarks, and to report to the Security Council every 120 days on the implementation of the mandate, including on progress in the protection of civilians, the peace process, the extension of State authority, security sector reform and accountability;
13. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

